WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:03.167
- [Lisa] Think of being in
a freezer and then somebody

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:08.101
puts your face directly into
a big pile of wet ice cubes.

00:00:08.101 --> 00:00:09.909
(playful music)

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:12.076
That's what it feels like.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.644
- [Narrator] I'm speaking to Lisa Kelly.

00:00:15.644 --> 00:00:17.978
She's a scuba diver
with National Geographic,

00:00:17.978 --> 00:00:22.645
and she leads expeditions in
Antarctica and the Arctic.

00:00:22.645 --> 00:00:24.851
(water splashes)

00:00:24.851 --> 00:00:26.987
Temperatures in arctic
waters typically range

00:00:26.987 --> 00:00:30.552
between 25 and 35 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:00:30.552 --> 00:00:32.024
Because the waters are so cold,

00:00:32.024 --> 00:00:33.963
divers have to use special gear.

00:00:33.963 --> 00:00:36.401
This includes the use of a dry suit.

00:00:36.401 --> 00:00:38.201
These suits actually keep divers dry

00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:39.791
when they're underwater.

00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:42.612
- [Lisa] I like to call
it a self-induced state

00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:47.512
of hypothermia, really,
because even though you're dry,

00:00:47.512 --> 00:00:50.043
you're still going to get cold.

00:00:50.043 --> 00:00:53.886
And that is such a shock
on peoples' system.

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:56.115
Unfortunately there have been deaths due

00:00:56.115 --> 00:00:59.888
to cardiac incidents because of that.

00:00:59.888 --> 00:01:01.955
- [Narrator] Aside from the
cold, there are other things

00:01:01.955 --> 00:01:04.111
that divers need to be on the lookout for.

00:01:04.111 --> 00:01:04.944
(walrus roar)

00:01:04.944 --> 00:01:08.395
- [Lisa] Walrus and to a
certain extent, polar bears.

00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:09.428
(bear roar)

00:01:09.428 --> 00:01:12.319
Animals like killer
whales reverberate sound,

00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:16.499
we call it pinging, it sounds
like a gunshot under water.

00:01:16.499 --> 00:01:19.367
It can go right through you.

00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:20.841
(atmospheric music)

00:01:20.841 --> 00:01:22.304
- [Narrator] But Lisa
Kelly says that diving

00:01:22.304 --> 00:01:24.382
in the Antarctic and the Arctic

00:01:24.382 --> 00:01:26.879
is an unparalleled experience.

00:01:26.879 --> 00:01:29.746
And it starts on the boat.

00:01:29.746 --> 00:01:31.917
- [Lisa] That initial
feeling when you fall back

00:01:31.917 --> 00:01:35.458
in the water, it's just
the sound of you breathing

00:01:35.458 --> 00:01:37.757
through your regulator.

00:01:37.757 --> 00:01:41.391
It suddenly becomes your own little world.

00:01:41.391 --> 00:01:43.760
All of a sudden, all these amazing colors

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:44.793
start to pop out.

00:01:44.793 --> 00:01:48.206
It really is a whole other universe.

00:01:48.206 --> 00:01:51.341
And in Antarctica, you're
there in a place where possibly

00:01:51.341 --> 00:01:53.758
nobody has ever dived before.

